Bloom␀␀ÄñFilters out pixels under this level of brightness. Value is in gamma-space.ˆ^␀␀␀␀:Strength of the bloom filter.ˆ^␀␀␀␀DSet the radius of the bloom effectˆ^␀␀␀␀Å»Set the maximum intensity that Unity uses to calculate Bloom. If pixels in your Scene are more intense than this, URP renders them at their current intensity, but uses this intensity value for the purposes of Bloom calculations.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄéUse the color picker to select a color for the Bloom effect to tint to.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Å Use bicubic sampling instead of bilinear sampling for the upsampling passes. This is slightly more expensive but helps getting smoother visuals.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄíThe number of final iterations to skip in the effect processing sequence.ˆ^␀␀˘^␀␀␀␀zDirtiness texture to add smudges or dust to the bloom effect.␀␀Lens Dirtˆ^␀␀␀␀(Amount of dirtiness.ˆ^␀␀␀␀nModify influence of the red channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀rModify influence of the green channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀pModify influence of the blue channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀nModify influence of the red channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀rModify influence of the green channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀pModify influence of the blue channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀nModify influence of the red channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀rModify influence of the green channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀pModify influence of the blue channel in the overall mix.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄåUse the slider to set the strength of the Chromatic Aberration effect.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÅFAdjusts the overall exposure of the scene in EV100. This is applied after HDR effect and right before tonemapping so it won't affect previous effects in the chain.ˆ^␀␀␀␀jExpands or shrinks the overall range of tonal values.ˆ^␀␀␀␀NTint the render by multiplying a color.ˆ^␀␀␀␀8Shift the hue of all colors.ˆ^␀␀␀␀FPushes the intensity of all colors.ˆ^␀␀␀␀fA 2D Lookup Texture (LUT) to use for color grading.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄñHow much of the lookup texture will contribute to the color grading effect.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄËUse "Gaussian" for a faster but non physical depth of field; "Bokeh" for a more realistic but slower depth of field.ˆ^␀␀␀␀\The distance at which the blurring will start.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄÇThe distance at which the blurring will reach its maximum radius.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä¥The maximum radius of the gaussian blur. Values above 1 may show under-sampling artifacts.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä≤Use higher quality sampling to reduce flickering and improve the overall blur smoothness.ˆ^␀␀␀␀FThe distance to the point of focus.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄÊThe ratio of aperture (known as f-stop or f-number). The smaller the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä“The distance between the lens and the film. The larger the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.ˆ^␀␀␀␀<The number of aperture blades.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Å,The curvature of aperture blades. The smaller the value is, the more visible aperture blades are. A value of 1 will make the bokeh perfectly circular.ˆ^␀␀␀␀VThe rotation of aperture blades in degrees.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄƒThe type of grain to use. You can select a preset or provide your own texture by selecting Custom.ˆ^␀␀␀␀xUse the slider to set the strength of the Film Grain effect.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä⁄Controls the noisiness response curve based on scene luminance. Higher values mean less noise in light areas.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä∏A tileable texture to use for the grain. The neutral value is 0.5 where no grain is applied.ˆ^␀␀␀␀0Total distortion amount.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄûIntensity multiplier on X axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄûIntensity multiplier on Y axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.ˆ^␀␀␀␀pDistortion center point. 0.5,0.5 is center of the screenˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄÏControls global screen scaling for the distortion effect. Use this to hide screen borders when using high "Intensity."ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä Use this to control and apply a hue to the dark tones. This has a more exaggerated effect on shadows.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä¢Use this to control and apply a hue to the mid-range tones with a power function.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä†Use this to increase and apply a hue to the signal and make highlights brighter.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄÍThe motion blur technique to use. If you don't need object motion blur, CameraOnly will result in better performance.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄÿThe quality of the effect. Lower presets will result in better performance at the expense of visual quality.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄòThe strength of the motion blur filter. Acts as a multiplier for velocities.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÅRSets the maximum length, as a fraction of the screen's full resolution, that the velocity resulting from Camera rotation can have. Lower values will improve performance.ˆ^␀␀␀␀6Panini projection distance.ˆ^␀␀␀␀<Panini projection crop to fit.ˆ^␀␀␀␀fUse this to control and apply a hue to the shadows.ˆ^␀␀␀␀hUse this to control and apply a hue to the midtones.ˆ^␀␀␀␀lUse this to control and apply a hue to the highlights.˘^␀␀ˆ^␀␀␀␀Shadow Limits␀␀vStart point of the transition between shadows and midtones.ˆ^␀␀␀␀rEnd point of the transition between shadows and midtones.˘^␀␀ˆ^␀␀␀␀ Highlight Limits␀␀|Start point of the transition between midtones and highlights.ˆ^␀␀␀␀xEnd point of the transition between midtones and highlights.ˆ^␀␀␀␀:The color to use for shadows.ˆ^␀␀␀␀@The color to use for highlights.ˆ^␀␀␀␀rBalance between the colors in the highlights and shadows.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄàSelect a tonemapping algorithm to use for the color grading process.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Vignette color.ˆ^␀␀␀␀xSets the vignette center point (screen center is [0.5,0.5]).ˆ^␀␀␀␀>Amount of vignetting on screen.ˆ^␀␀␀␀FSmoothness of the vignette borders.ˆ^␀␀␀␀Ä¶Should the vignette be perfectly round or be dependent on the current aspect ratio?ˆ^␀␀␀␀jSets the white balance to a custom color temperature.ˆ^␀␀␀␀ÄÇSets the white balance to compensate for a green or magenta tint.Ú␀␀ü␀␀␀␀Uº6␀␀Ä–Obsolete, to match the capital from 'Prepass' to 'PrePass' (UnityUpgradable) -> BeforeRe